From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Laferriere <9342524+samlaf@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:15:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/acpi: let the BadAML sandbox read UEFI runtime data

Azure's AML reads the hardware configuration its UEFI firmware leaves in
a SystemMemory region named BIOS (0xBFF86000 in the BadAML paper's
survey). That region is EfiRuntimeServicesData: guest memory, so private,
and the sandbox denies every read of it, returning 0.

The BadAML paper's sandbox allows exactly this, as its Rule 2: reads of
EfiRuntimeServicesData, found through the EFI memory map, with writes
still denied (Takekoshi et al., CCS 2025, section 6.2,
https://doi.org/10.1145/3719027.3765123). The Contrast port this builds
on dropped the rule because Contrast runs on bare metal
(https://katexochen.aro.bz/posts/badaml/).

Restore it. A malicious host can use it to read firmware data in that
region, as the paper notes, but not to write it, so it opens no path to
code execution.

Signed-off-by: Samuel Laferriere <samlaf92@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/acpi/acpica/sandbox.h | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/acpi/acpica/sandbox.h b/drivers/acpi/acpica/sandbox.h
--- a/drivers/acpi/acpica/sandbox.h
+++ b/drivers/acpi/acpica/sandbox.h
@@ -111,6 +111,14 @@
 		return true;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Azure's AML reads configuration the firmware leaves in UEFI runtime
+	 * services data, which is private memory. Reads are all it needs.
+	 */
+	if (is_read && IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_EFI) &&
+	    efi_mem_type(phys_addr) == EFI_RUNTIME_SERVICES_DATA)
+		return true;
+
 	__sandbox_access_log.access_allowed = false;
 	return false;
 }
